    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §366-a, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4830\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the social services law, in relation to\nproviding a model health care proxy form, living will or other\ncomparable materials to individuals applying for medical\nassistance\n\nPURPOSE:\nThe purpose of this legislation is to require that individuals\napplying for medical assistance be provided with a model proxy form.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nThis bill would add new subdivision l-a of section 366-a of the social\nservices law.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\nIndividuals deserve to have their health care wishes honored, yet a\nlarge majority of New York residents do not have a health care proxy\nfor times when they cannot express their health care preferences. As\na result, their wishes are often unknown, and they might be given\nmedical treatment that they would not want or not be given treatment\nthat matches their choices. Requiring that individuals applying for\nmedical assistance be provided with a model proxy form and other\nimportant health care-related documents will increase the awareness\nof these documents and spur more New Yorkers to complete them.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nIndividuals deserve to have their health care wishes honored, yet a\nlarge majority of New York residents do not have a health care proxy\nfor times when they cannot express their health care preferences.\nAs a result, their wishes are often unknown, and they might be\ngiven medical treatment that they would not want or not be given\ntreatment that matches their choices. Requiring that individuals\napplying for medical assistance be provided with a model proxy form\nand other important health care-related documents will increase the\nawareness of these documents and spur more New Yorkers to complete\nthem.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n\n",
